# Shinobi — current state

Updated: 2026-09-25

M2 consumes Codejitsu's ApplicationResolver and EffectiveApplication. Generic App
inheritance and Spec conformance belong to Codejitsu; Shinobi owns deployment
bindings, node lifecycle, HTTP request/response values and handler execution.
The older Shinobi Application/Repository/Resolver classes remain legacy code.

## Verified POC

- Shinobi App Scroll resolves with spec://shinobi/app#1.0.0 and runtime capability.
- OpenSwoole serves the app through host/port deployment bindings.
- Environment listener overrides work; address is listener metadata, not a route predicate.
- HTTP routing uses the listening port rather than trusting the Host header's port.
- Handler exceptions return a generic 500 without killing the request worker.
- An isolated ArchIQ-shaped child fixture inherits the spec/runtime, mounts via
  deployment bindings, and executes its own handler.
- Composer test: 15 tests, 51 assertions on PHP 8.5.4 with OpenSwoole.

## Constraints and next slice

One HTTP listener only. Array port bindings support matching but do not create
additional sockets. No NATS, dynamic mount lifecycle, or production deployment
claim. Composer currently requires a sibling Codejitsu main workspace; see README.

Next: manually confirm the merged default HTTP route, then define the concrete
Shinobi app contract and ArchIQ App Scroll. Keep product behavior out of the node;
mount ArchIQ through the same generic composition and deployment path.
